<figure class="protocol-demo">
  <figcaption>
    <span class="eyebrow">Illustration of the repair</span>
    <p>
      Two synthetic file reads from the PR’s documented example, shown as
      simplified protocol before the repair and assistant text afterward. This
      is an explanation, not a live model execution.
    </p>
  </figcaption>
  <div class="protocol-comparison">
    <details open>
      <summary>Before · incompatible tool protocol</summary>
      <pre><code>{`assistant.tool_calls:
  id: callmw2u3tq3 / name: read
  {"path":"notes.txt","optional":true}
  id: calllu52e2fv / name: read
  {"path":"readme.md","optional":true}

tool / tool_call_id: callmw2u3tq3
  NOTES_TOKEN=amber-17
tool / tool_call_id: calllu52e2fv
  README_TOKEN=violet-29`}</code></pre>
      <p>A strict no-tools contract can reject historical tool fields.</p>
    </details>
    <details open>
      <summary>After · evidence as assistant text</summary>
      <pre><code>{`assistant.content:
  [tool call id=callmw2u3tq3 name=read]
  {"path":"notes.txt","optional":true}
  [tool call id=calllu52e2fv name=read]
  {"path":"readme.md","optional":true}
  [tool result id=callmw2u3tq3 name=read]
  NOTES_TOKEN=amber-17
  [tool result id=calllu52e2fv name=read]
  README_TOKEN=violet-29`}</code></pre>
      <p>
        The association survives. Stored conversation history stays unchanged.
      </p>
    </details>
  </div>
  <a class="text-link" href="https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/148106">
    Inspect the merged implementation ↗
  </a>
</figure>
